<h1>Metal Detectors &amp; Inspection Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor-mounted inspection systems scan every product on the packaging line for contaminants, weight deviations, label defects, and package integrity failures, then trigger <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject mechanisms</a> to remove non-conforming product before it reaches the consumer. The four primary inspection technologies are metal detectors, X-ray systems, checkweighers, and vision inspection. Mettler-Toledo, Loma Systems, Fortress Technology, Ishida, Eriez, and Cognex manufacture inspection systems with integrated conveyor and reject hardware for food, pharmaceutical, and consumer goods packaging lines.</p>
<h2 id="metal-heading">Conveyor Metal Detectors</h2>
<p>Conveyor metal detectors pass product through a balanced coil aperture that generates an electromagnetic field. Metal contamination, ferrous, non-ferrous, or stainless steel, disturbs the field, triggering a detection signal. The detector sends a reject command to the downstream reject mechanism after calculating the delay for product travel from the aperture to the reject point.</p>
<p>Detection sensitivity depends on aperture size, product effect (the electromagnetic signal the product itself generates), and contaminant type. Ferrous metals are easiest to detect, fragments as small as 0.5mm in diameter in non-conductive products. Non-ferrous metals (aluminum, copper) require 1.0-1.5mm minimum. Stainless steel 316 is the most difficult, 1.5-2.5mm minimum for reliable detection. Wet, salty, or conductive products (fresh meat, cheese, salty snacks) generate high product effect that masks small contaminant signals, requiring advanced multi-frequency or multi-simultaneous-frequency (MSF) detectors.</p>
<p>Mettler-Toledo's Profile Advantage, Loma Systems' IQ4 series, and Fortress Technology's Interceptor line represent current multi-frequency metal detection technology for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging lines</a>. These systems automatically adjust frequency and sensitivity based on product characteristics, reducing false rejects while maintaining detection performance.</p>
<p>Metal detector conveyor integration requires a metal-free zone around the aperture, no metal conveyor components within the aperture area. The conveyor belt uses non-metallic splices (sewn or plastic pin). The conveyor frame transitions from steel to plastic or stainless steel through the detection zone. Most inspection manufacturers supply the conveyor section as part of the integrated metal detector system, ensuring proper metal-free zone design.</p>
<h2 id="xray-heading">X-Ray Inspection Systems</h2>
<p>X-ray inspection systems use low-energy X-ray beams to create a density image of every product on the conveyor. The X-ray image reveals contaminants that differ in density from the product, metal, glass, stone, bone, ceramic, dense plastics, and rubber. Software algorithms analyze the image in real time (5-20ms per product) and trigger rejection when a contaminant is detected.</p>
<p>X-ray systems detect contaminant types that metal detectors miss. Glass fragments in glass jars, stone chips in grain products, bone fragments in meat, and rubber gasket pieces in sauced products are invisible to metal detectors but visible on X-ray. X-ray systems also perform secondary inspection functions, fill level verification, missing component detection (e.g., a missing prize in a cereal box), mass estimation, and seal integrity checking.</p>
<p>Mettler-Toledo, Ishida, Eagle Product Inspection (a Mettler-Toledo brand), and Eriez manufacture X-ray inspection systems for packaging conveyor lines. Systems range from compact single-beam units for individual product inspection to dual-beam and side-shoot configurations for detecting contaminants in tall or irregularly shaped products.</p>
<p>X-ray systems require radiation safety compliance, shielding, interlocks, warning labels, and periodic radiation surveys. Modern food-grade X-ray systems are fully shielded and emit no measurable radiation outside the cabinet. The product receives a dose far below any regulatory limit. However, facility safety officers must register X-ray equipment and maintain inspection records per state and federal radiation safety regulations.</p>
<p>Relative cost for X-ray systems is $$$ to $$$$, significantly more than metal detectors ($ to $$). The higher cost is justified when the contamination risk includes non-metallic foreign objects or when secondary inspection functions (fill level, component counting) replace separate inspection stations. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for inspection system cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="checkweigh-heading">In-Motion Checkweighers</h2>
<p>Checkweighers weigh every product in motion on the conveyor without stopping the line. A precision load cell under a short conveyor section captures the product weight as it crosses the weigh zone. Products outside the target weight range (underweight or overweight) trigger a reject signal. Checkweighers verify fill accuracy, detect missing components, and ensure regulatory weight compliance (NIST Handbook 133, EU Prepackages Directive).</p>
<p>Checkweigher accuracy depends on product speed, vibration isolation, and weigh zone length. Typical accuracy is ±0.1g to ±1g depending on product weight and belt speed. Higher speeds reduce weighing time per product, decreasing accuracy. The weigh zone must be long enough for the product to settle on the load cell, typically 1.5 to 2 times the product length. Products must arrive with controlled spacing, only one product on the weigh section at a time. An <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> or speed-up belt upstream creates the required gap.</p>
<p>Mettler-Toledo, Ishida, Wipotec-OCS, and Minebea Intec manufacture high-speed checkweighers with integrated conveyor and reject systems. Throughput ranges from 50 to 600+ products per minute depending on product size and weight range. High-speed pharmaceutical checkweighers (Wipotec, Mettler-Toledo) verify individual blister pack weights at 400+ per minute with accuracy of ±0.05g.</p>
<p>Checkweighers integrate with upstream filling equipment through feedback control. The checkweigher's statistical weight data feeds back to the filler's PLC, allowing automatic fill weight adjustment to maintain target weight and minimize overfill giveaway. A filler consistently producing packages 2g heavy wastes product worth thousands of dollars per shift, checkweigher feedback eliminates this giveaway.</p>
<h2 id="vision-heading">Vision Inspection Systems</h2>
<p>Vision inspection systems use industrial cameras and image processing software to inspect product appearance, label accuracy, package integrity, and dimensional conformance at line speed. A camera captures an image of each product as it passes a fixed position on the conveyor. The software analyzes the image against trained criteria and sends a pass/fail signal to the reject controller within 5-50ms.</p>
<p>Common vision inspection checks on packaging conveyors include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Label presence and position:</strong> Confirms a label exists and is positioned within tolerance. Detects missing labels, skewed labels, and wrinkled labels.</li>
<li><strong>Label content verification:</strong> OCR reads text on labels (lot codes, date codes, ingredient lists) and verifies correctness. Catches wrong-product labels and expired date codes.</li>
<li><strong>Barcode and 2D code readability:</strong> Scans barcodes and QR codes to verify readability and correct data. Unreadable codes cause retailer chargebacks and shipping rejections.</li>
<li><strong>Cap and seal integrity:</strong> Detects missing caps, crooked caps, broken tamper bands, and incomplete heat seals. Critical for product safety and shelf life.</li>
<li><strong>Fill level:</strong> Measures visible fill level through transparent or translucent containers. Detects underfilled and overfilled packages.</li>
<li><strong>Color and surface defects:</strong> Detects discoloration, surface damage, foreign material on the product surface, and cosmetic defects that indicate quality problems.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cognex, Keyence, Omron (Microscan), and SICK manufacture vision systems for packaging conveyor applications. Cognex's In-Sight series and Keyence's CV-X series are widely deployed on food and pharmaceutical packaging lines. Smart cameras integrate the camera, processor, and I/O in a single unit, simplifying installation on existing conveyors.</p>
<p>Vision system integration requires controlled lighting (LED bar lights, dome lights, or backlight panels), a fixed camera mount position relative to the conveyor, and a product trigger sensor that tells the camera when to capture. Product tracking from the camera position to the <a href="/conveyor-functions/pusher-diverter/">reject mechanism</a> uses an encoder on the conveyor to calculate when the defective product reaches the reject point.</p>
<h2 id="placement-heading">Where to Place Inspection Systems</h2>
<p>Inspection system placement follows two principles: <strong>inspect as early as possible</strong> (to prevent defective product from wasting downstream processing) and <strong>inspect as late as possible</strong> (to catch contamination introduced by downstream equipment). Best practice uses both, early inspection catches incoming defects, final inspection catches everything.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>After filling, before sealing:</strong> Checkweigher verifies fill weight. Rejects underweight and overweight before the package is sealed, allowing product rework (refill underweight, reduce overfill).</li>
<li><strong>After labeling:</strong> Vision system verifies label presence, position, content, and barcode readability. Rejects mislabeled product before case packing.</li>
<li><strong>Before case packing:</strong> Metal detector scans individual products at maximum sensitivity (small aperture). Individual product inspection detects smaller contaminants than case-level inspection.</li>
<li><strong>After case packing (final inspection):</strong> X-ray or metal detector scans the sealed case as the last check before palletizing. Catches contamination introduced during case packing. Some retailers require this final inspection point specifically.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every inspection point requires a <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> immediately downstream. The conveyor design at each inspection station must provide controlled product spacing, consistent belt speed, and adequate clearance for the reject mechanism. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> upstream of inspection stations ensure steady product flow for reliable detection.</p>
<h2 id="conveyor-heading">Conveyor Requirements</h2>
<p>Inspection systems impose specific requirements on the conveyor sections that carry product through the inspection zone:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistent speed:</strong> VFD-controlled motors with encoder feedback maintain belt speed within ±1%. Speed variation causes checkweigher inaccuracy and reject timing errors. The conveyor speed signal feeds the inspection system's product tracking calculation.</li>
<li><strong>Product spacing:</strong> Checkweighers require one product on the weigh cell at a time. Metal detectors and X-ray systems need sufficient gap between products to assign reject signals to individual products. Minimum spacing depends on the inspection system, typically 1-2 product lengths between products.</li>
<li><strong>Vibration isolation:</strong> Checkweighers require vibration-free mounting. The weigh conveyor section isolates from the main conveyor frame using rubber mounts or spring isolators. Adjacent equipment vibration (compressors, other conveyors) transmits through the floor and degrades weighing accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Metal-free zones:</strong> Metal detector conveyor sections eliminate metal from the detection zone, non-metallic belt splices, plastic idler rollers, and stainless steel or plastic frame components within the aperture. The metal-free zone extends 12-18 inches on each side of the aperture.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitary construction:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Food-grade inspection conveyors</a> use stainless steel frames, FDA-compliant belts, IP69K motors, and open-frame designs for washdown cleaning. The inspection equipment itself must match the environmental rating.</li>
</ul>
